How to build a Greenfoot release
================================

NOTE: This process changed when the build moved to Gradle and the installers started being
built by GitHub Actions, from the repo root. Local Ant builds are no longer how releases are
made. Greenfoot and BlueJ are built and released together from the same
version.properties change and the same "Build Installers" workflow run.


Before build:

 1. Check the copyright years (for the "About" dialog). The line to be updated is
    'menu.help.copyright.line0', which is in the labels file:
    'labels/english/greenfoot/greenfoot-labels'.

 2. Update the Greenfoot version number in version.properties (repo root):
       greenfoot_major / greenfoot_minor / greenfoot_release / greenfoot_suffix /
       greenfoot_rcnumber
    If there are API changes, make sure that:
    - greenfoot_api_breaking has also been updated if the change is breaking (see the
      comment above it in version.properties)
    - the labels have been updated with a description of the API changes
    - the @version javadoc tag has been correctly set for any user-visible Greenfoot
      classes which are affected by the API changes.
    If you bump to a new x.y.z, add a new GUID for it in version.properties (in the
    "Greenfoot GUIDs" section) - get one from guidgen.com.

 3. Test bundled scenarios and make sure they are compatible with the new API version.

 4. Commit and push the version.properties change (and any label/copyright changes) to main.


Build process (GitHub Actions):

 5. Pushing the version.properties change automatically triggers the "Build Installers"
    workflow (.github/workflows/build-installers.yml) - see bluej/doc/HOWTO.make-release for
    the full description, since it builds BlueJ and Greenfoot installers together in the same
    run. You can also trigger it manually from the Actions tab if needed.

 6. Download the greenfoot-installers-windows, greenfoot-installers-other,
    greenfoot-installers-mac-*, and greenfoot-installers-arm64 artifacts from the completed
    workflow run.

 7. Test. If you find problems, fix them, bump greenfoot_rcnumber in version.properties,
    push, and repeat from step 5.


Release:

 8. When happy, do a final version.properties change dropping the RC suffix, commit and push.

 9. Tag the released commit in git as GREENFOOT-RELEASE-x.y.z (e.g. GREENFOOT-RELEASE-3.9.1)
    and push the tag. Pushing a tag matching GREENFOOT-RELEASE-* (or BLUEJ-RELEASE-* for
    BlueJ) triggers the "Build Installers" workflow again, and its final job,
    "Publish-Release", automatically collects the built greenfoot-installers-* files (already
    named with version and architecture) and creates a DRAFT GitHub Release for the tag with
    them attached. No manual downloading/renaming/re-uploading of artifacts is needed.

10. Go to the repository's Releases page, open the new draft, write the release notes,
    double-check the attached files (Greenfoot-windows-x.y.z.msi, .zip,
    Greenfoot-linux-x64-x.y.z.deb, Greenfoot-linux-arm64-x.y.z.deb,
    Greenfoot-generic-x.y.z.jar, Greenfoot-mac-x64-x.y.z.dmg, Greenfoot-mac-aarch64-x.y.z.dmg),
    and click "Publish release".
    Source is available at the tag; there's no separate source-zip upload, and no manual
    copying of files to a greenfoot.org download server any more - GitHub Releases is the
    download location.

11. Create new version in bug tracker.

12. Update web pages (see below).

If necessary:
13. If the API version has changed, copy Greenfoot-core-X.X.X-jar to the gallery:
    /home/mygame/mygame/public/sharedjars/
14. If any of the 3rd party libraries have changed copy them to:
    /home/mygame/mygame/public/sharedjars/



Web-pages to update on www.greenfoot.org.
These need uploading files to the greenfoot home folder:
  (/home/greenfoot/www/download/files/javadoc)
    update javadoc with the generated doc from create-api-doc
These pages can be edited directly:
  download
  download/installation
  site/download_source
  download/old-downloads.html
  version_history
